## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed by metal ions or clusters with organic ligands, have shown great potential in gas storage and separation, luminescence, catalysis, drug delivery, sensing, and so on. More than 20,000 MOFs have been reported by adjusting the composition and reaction conditions, and most of them were synthesized by hydrothermal or solvothermal methods. The conventional solvothermal methods are favorable for the slow crystallization of MOFs to obtain single crystals or highly crystalline powders, which are suitable for the structure analysis. However, their harsh synthesis conditions, long reaction time, and difficulty in continuous synthesis limit their scale-up in industrial production and application. Meanwhile, shaping or processing is also required to bring MOF crystals and powders into the market. Therefore, this review demonstrates the crystallization mechanisms of MOFs to understand how the synthetic parameters affect the final products. Additionally, a variety of promising synthetic routes which can be used for large scale synthesis were reviewed in details. Lastly, the prospects of MOF shaping and processing are provided to promote their industrial application.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, synthetic methods, crystallization mechanisms, scale-up
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), with highly crystalline and permanent porosity, are a unique category of material chemistry and have attracted appreciable attention during the past decades. Constructed by joining metal ions/clusters and organic linkers, which shows excellent structural tunability and diversity, MOFs exhibit great potential in gas storage [1, 2] and separation [3–5], luminescence [6], heterogeneous catalysis [7–9], drug delivery [10–12], sensing [13], etc. However, the structure of MOF products is hard to predict due to multiple factors influencing the synthetic route. The diversity of MOF structures derived from the forming units and the connections between the inorganic and organic part. Besides, selecting different methods with the same starting materials can obtain different MOF products with different sizes, and/or morphologies, even crystal structures. Therefore, we demonstrate the nucleation and crystallization mechanisms, and the effect of the modulator on the process of MOF synthesis in the beginning of this review to understand how the synthetic parameters affect the final products.Although a variety of MOFs have been reported, few of them can be manufactured at the required scale, price, and purity for real industrial applications. Hydrothermal (HT)/solvothermal (ST) methods, which are the most commonly used, can obtain single crystals and highly crystalline powders suitable for structural analysis. However, they are difficult to apply to large-scale MOF fabrication due to the long reaction time and harsh synthetic conditions. Therefore, various MOF synthetic methods which are suitable for scale-up have been considered, including microwave (MW), ultrasonic (US), mechanical, electrochemical (EC) methods, etc. These methods achieve the preparation of MOFs through different energy conversion pathways and show great potential for addressing the challenges that hinder the industrial process of MOF products.This review focuses on the formation mechanism of MOFs and highlights the promising new synthetic methods, such as hot-pressing method (HoP method), spray-drying (SD) synthesis, and continuous flow (CF) synthesis, which are the facile, rapid, and continuous methods that can be utilized in scale-up mass production for industrial applications. These methods will be discussed in detail by the definitions, synthetic routes, advantages, limitations, and several latest examples. Lastly, a summary and perspective on further synthetic strategies will be provided.
Although a variety of MOFs have been reported, few of them can be manufactured at the required scale, price, and purity for real industrial applications. Hydrothermal (HT)/solvothermal (ST) methods, which are the most commonly used, can obtain single crystals and highly crystalline powders suitable for structural analysis. However, they are difficult to apply to large-scale MOF fabrication due to the long reaction time and harsh synthetic
conditions. Therefore, various MOF synthetic methods which are suitable for scale-up have been considered, including microwave (MW),  ultrasonic  (US),  mechanical,  electrochemical  (EC) methods, etc. These methods achieve the preparation of MOFs through different energy conversion pathways and show great potential for addressing the challenges that hinder the industrial process of MOF products.
This review focuses on the formation mechanism of MOFs and highlights the promising new synthetic methods, such as hotpressing method (HoP method), spray-drying (SD) synthesis, and continuous flow (CF) synthesis, which are the facile, rapid, and continuous methods that can be utilized in scale-up mass production for industrial applications. These methods will be discussed in detail by the definitions, synthetic routes, advantages, limitations, and several latest examples. Lastly, a summary and perspective on further synthetic strategies will be provided.
## 2 Overview of MOF crystallization
Understanding crystallization is a perpetual challenge for chemists and materials scientists. Numerous theories, hypotheses, and models have been proposed to recognize the assembly and formation of MOFs. Herein, we adopt the wildly accepted LaMer's model to explain the process of crystallization [14]. As shown in Fig. 2, there are two main steps, which are called nucleation and crystal growth, respectively. In this section, we will discuss these
two procedures in MOF synthesis to gain a deeper understanding of MOF fabrication.
## 2.1 Nucleation
There are two types of nucleation: primary and secondary nucleation. The primary nucleation type is divided into homogeneous nucleation and heterogeneous nucleation. Homogeneous nucleation, which is described by classic nucleation theory (CNT), is the initial process where the condensed system is generated by the supersaturation of reaction solutions [16]. CNT demonstrates that crystallization is thermodynamically favorable under supersaturation conditions, and that nucleation begins with changes in the Gibbs free energy \((\Delta G)\), which is dependent on two contending factors. A negative contribution, which is referred to as \(\Delta G_{\nu}\), is derived from the liquid coagulation into solid nuclei, and the positive contribution, \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{s}}\), is attributed to the unfavorable free energy between liquid nuclei and solid nuclei. These two terms affect the free energy as follows
\[
\Delta G=4\pi r^2\Delta G_{\mathrm{s}}-\frac{4}{3}\pi r^3\Delta G_{\mathrm{v}}
\]
where \(r\) is the radius of the nucleus. The value of Gibbs free energy varies with the radius; once the critical value \(r_{\mathrm{c}}\) is reached, the growth of nuclei will be favorable and finally lead to the fabrication of bulk solids [17].
As suggested by CNT, a considerable supersaturation is
necessary for the nucleation process due to the similar probability of each nucleus in metastable liquid. However, the supersaturation needed for nucleation is much lower than the value predicted by CNT in many systems. Further study revealed that, when the systems contain foreign media, such as impurities, dust, bubbles, and/or solid interfaces, nucleation will occur at the foreign media, thereby reducing the obstacles arising from the increased surface energy. This process is defined as heterogeneous nucleation [18].
Secondary nucleation is a process where nucleation generates preexisting nuclei and/or crystals, which are referred to as crystal seeds [19]. For example, Zhou and co-workers reported a facile and rapid seed-mediated method for the synthesis of phase-pure Zr-porphyrinic MOFs by introducing seeds of the pre-synthesized MOFs into the reaction system (Fig. 3) [20]. Heterogeneous and secondary nucleation are widely utilized under industrial conditions due to the more accessible reaction conditions compared with homogeneous nucleation. Liu and co-authors used zeolite crystals as seeds to fabricate MOFs including MIL-101(Cr), MIL-100(Cr), MIL-104(Co), and MIL-53(Fe). Those nanosized zeolite crystal seeds can accelerate the nucleation step of MOF synthesis due to the enormous surface areas and shorten the reaction time by \(75\%\) [21]. Mitsuka and co-workers obtained UiO-66 via the seed-assisted spray-drying method for the first time. The experiment result demonstrated that MOF seeds play an important role in the nucleation process in MOF formation [22]. Those examples showed that heterogeneous nucleation and secondary nucleation by seed-assisted synthesis can shorten the reaction time and reduce the obstacles in MOF nucleation which makes MOF products more suitable for industrial application.
Recently, a few attempts emerged using in situ strategy with atomic or molecular-scale resolution to study the nucleation stage of MOF formation. Schu¨th's team observed discrete chemical species during zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) nucleation by using electrospray ionization mass spectrometry (ESI-MS). They can identify those species in different nucleation steps and explore their nucleation rates [23].
Patterson and co-workers used liquid cell transmission electron microscopy (LCTEM) to observe MOF nucleation and crystallization for the first time. The growth rates of UiO-66 and ZIF-8 particles were observed directly by image analysis, and meanwhile, the crystal structure can be determined by electron diffraction [24]. Similarly, Mirsaidov's team used this LCTEM technology to show the three steps of ZIF-8 nanocrystal nucleation process: (1) division into solute-poor and solute-rich regions on liquid–liquid phase; (2) aggregation on solute-rich regions; (3)
crystallization to form MOFs. Such nucleation pathway observation is shown in Fig. 4 [25].
Nakamura's group explored the formation of MOF-2 and MOF-5 through single-molecule atomic-resolution real-time electron microscopic (SMART-EM) technology. Two different types of single prenucleation clusters (PNCs) which can be observed by video imaging at 95 and \(120~^{\circ}\!C\) demonstrated that the structural bifurcation of MOF-2 and MOF-5 takes place at the PCNs stage [26].
## 2.2 Crystal growth
Crystal growth is a thermodynamically favorable process under supersaturation conditions. In general, larger particles are favored under equilibrium conditions, which leads to the dissolution of smaller particles, due to the minimization of the total surface free energy [18]. As shown in Fig. 5, the classical growth models can be divided into three stages. (1) Under lower supersaturation conditions, the crystal surface is usually smooth, in which the crystal growth tends to occur by attachment growth units into energetically favored sites. This stage is referred to as "spiral
growth" [27]. (2) At a higher supersaturation level, the crystal growth tends to cause two-dimensional (2D) nucleation, which means that the growth units begin to attach to the "step" sites through a "birth and spread" mechanism [18]. (3) Eventually, the growth units commence to integrate with random sites on the surface, and generate various shaped crystals [28].
The classical growth models highlight two hypotheses. First, the growing nuclei retain the same crystalline phase as the final products. Second, the crystal growth process occurs by attaching smaller growth units rather than by aggregation [18]. Nevertheless, both of these hypotheses can be overturned, especially under kinetic conditions. Non-classical growth models include Ostwald's step rule and aggregation. Ostwald's step rule means the crystallization of many systems through a sequential process, rather than a one-step procedure [30]. Compared with classical growth models, Ostwald claims that crystal growth initially occurs in the phase with the lowest free energy; rather than the thermodynamically most stable phase [31]. Further transformation is required to obtain thermodynamically stable products. Carreon and co-workers observed the structural
evolution of ZIF-8. The nucleation, crystallization, and growth periods were identified clearly, and the kinetics of transformation was elucidated. They claimed that the significantly increased average particle size during ZIF-8 crystallization was mostly due to the Ostwald ripening [32].
Considering the aggregation process in crystal growth is necessary and unavoidable, a comprehensive review of aggregation in solids has been published by De Yoreo et al. [33]. In addition, crystals that grow through aggregation-mediated pathways may exhibit morphologies different from those predicted by the classical growth model [18].
Very recently, Deng's group reported a thorough study on the evolution of MOF crystal interface growth by in-situ dark-field microscope (Fig. 6). The crystal growth rate at specific facets of five classical MOFs (1D, 2D, and 3D) was measured precisely, and the key determining factors of crystal interfacial growth were demonstrated clearly. Researchers extracted the effect of each building block by studying the mixture of MOF building blocks (metal ions and linkers). Consequently, they proposed a new growth mechanism of Cu-MOF-2-BDC and -NDC, and identified an unprecedented negative reaction order of organic linkers on HKUST-1 crystal growth. Moreover, the observed retreat and recovery of MOF crystal interface demonstrated the interfacial influence of MOF growth process [34].
## 2.3 Modulator
The utilization of modulators in synthesis to control the morphology and size of MOF crystals proves to be an efficient strategy. In certain cases, a modulator can change the acid or base equilibria of the reaction system and promote the formation of
coordination bonds. In other cases, the modulator acts as a competitor to organic linkers and affects the coordination reaction. As a result, either the nucleation process or the growth process of MOF crystals will be changed, which affects the final morphology and/or size of the crystals [35]. Here, the modulators can be divided into two different types, the non-coordinating modulators and the coordinating modulators.
The non-coordinating modulators include inorganic bases and organic bases, such as NaOH, trimethylamine, and n-butylamine. For instance, Chin and co-workers reported that synthesizing \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-53 in the presence of NaOH can yield nanoparticles [36]. The authors claimed that the increased pH value can promote the deprotonation of organic linkers, especially carboxylic linkers. Weaker bases, such as trimethylamine and n-butylamine, also trigger fast nucleation by deprotonation. An example is reported by Wiebcke who used n-butylamine as the modulator to adjust the crystal size of ZIF-8 from \(\sim10\,\mathrm{nm}\) to \(\sim1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) [37].
For coordination modulation, several modulators, including monocarboxylic acids and N-heterocyclic linkers, can be employed. The excess of coordinating modulators promotes the preferential reaction with metal ions. The exchange of the modulators with the organic linkers initiates the formation of crystalline MOFs [35]. The effect of benzoic acid and acetic acid on the crystal size of UiO series MOFs was demonstrated by Behrens's group in 2011 [38]. Polyacids can also be used as modulator to synthesize MOFs. Cohen and co-workers chose Poly(acrylic acid) (PAA) and poly(vinylbenzoic acid) (PBA) as modulator for the synthesis of UiO-66 [39]. Compared with other acid modulators, polymerized acids show a narrow MOF particle size distribution. Notably, this approach also led to the production of the first single crystal of Zr-based MOF.
The \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value of monocarboxylic acid and the acidity of reaction solution also played different roles in MOF fabrication. Deng's group explored the \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value of different modulators and their corresponding acidity in a series of Zr-based porphyrin MOFs. Researchers gave a phase diagram of PCN-221, PCN-222, PCN-223, and PCN-224 with the \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value and the acidic concentration variation (Fig. 7). They can also obtain the pure phase of PCN-223 by accurately controlling the synthetic conditions, for example, introducing high \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value acetic acid or 4-tert-butylbenzoic acid [40].
The successful application of coordination modulation for obtaining single crystals was not restricted to Zr-based MOFs. In the work of Liu et al. [41], single crystals of two \(\mathrm{Eu(III)/In(III)}\) - MOFs were synthesized by solvothermal reaction using 2- fluorobenzoic acid as the modulator. Henkelis et al. also prepared single crystals of CPO-27-Mg, CPO-27-20, and its isoreticular UTSA-74 by using salicylic acid and benzoic acid [42].
The coordination modulation method also controls the morphology of MOFs. Kitagawa's team investigated the growth mechanism of \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]_{n}\) through the modulating method [43]. They observed a morphological transition (from octahedron to cuboctahedron and cube) when the modulator concentration was increased. As shown in Fig. 8, the different concentrations of lauric acid, which acts as the modulator, endow the distinct attachment energy of standard units on each facet. In addition, this coarse-grain attachment process allowed the fabrication of crystal orientation on a bare substrate for the first time.
Certain N-heterocyclic modulators can also control the morphology and/or size of MOF particles. Our group presented the design and synthesis of a novel Ce(III)-MOF, BIT-58, which is constructed by \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) and 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (BTB) [44]. Both the crystal size and defects of this MOF were controlled by using N-heterocyclic modulators such as imidazole, 1- methylimidazole (1-mIM), and piperazine. The particle size of BIT
58 was reduced from \(\sim25~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) to \(\sim30~\mathrm{nm}\) without a decrease in crystallinity. Furthermore, the obtained BIT-58 nanoparticles can be easily coated or fabricated on thin films for catalysis processes (Fig. 9).
This method is also utilized in producing hierarchically porous MOFs since it can create defects in MOF structures. Wu and coworkers employed the high-resolution neutron powder diffraction technique and obtained direct evidence of defects in UiO-66 caused by the missing linker [45]. Cai et al. developed a simple and versatile, modulator-induced, defect-formation strategy to design hierarchically porous MOFs by using monocarboxylic acid as the modulator (Fig. 10) [46]. They claimed that owing to the unique structure, the modulator has a dual role in which the monocarboxylic group is coordinated to the metal ion, while the alkyl group creates defects. Another study from the same team showed a modulator-induced defect-construction by using acetic acid and investigated the effect of different defects on
photocatalysis [47].
Although defects can be created to deliver hierarchically porous MOFs, the increasing number of defects can also reduce the stability of MOFs, which could hamper further applications such as catalysis. Therefore, Feng and co-workers demonstrated a strategy that can simultaneously guarantee defect formation and stability. A hemilabile linker (4-sulfonatobenzoate) was introduced to the synthesis of UiO-66 and helped construct up to six defects per metal cluster in UiO-66 [48]. The 4-sulfonatobenzoate can not only create defects as a modulator, but also enhance the structural stability as a co-ligand.
## 3 Synthesis methods
A variety of synthetic methods can be utilized to design and synthesize MOFs [49–51]. Generally, different synthetic conditions should be cautiously chosen according to the various precursor systems and different purposes to ensure the successful formation and rearrangement of coordinate bonds. In this section, a detailed review of conventional solvothermal synthesis, modulator methods, microwave-assisted methods, sonochemical synthesis, electrochemical synthesis, mechanochemical (MC) synthesis, hot-pressing synthesis, spray-drying synthesis, and continuous flow synthesis is presented to highlight the fabrication of MOF materials. Table 1 makes a comparison of different methods for MOF synthesis. Table 2 presents various parameters of several typical MOFs synthesized by different methods and conditions to compare those synthetic methods.
## 3.1 Solvothermal synthesis
Conventional solvothermal synthesis is the most widely employed method to obtain MOFs. Although a uniform definition has not been established, here, we adopt the definition from Rebenau, which states that solvothermal synthesis is the reaction of mixing a solution of metal salts and organic ligands in a closed vessel under subsequent heating to obtain an insoluble framework [52]. Different from the slow diffusion method, in solvothermal synthesis, the sealed container allows the reaction system to be heated to a temperature and pressure above the boiling point of solvents. Under such conditions, the insoluble parts, especially the
organic linkers, can be partially or completely dissolved in the solvent.
Synthetic conditions, such as reaction temperature, reaction time, solvent, pH value, concentration, and ratio of metal precursor to ligand, can affect the crystallization process [35]. In addition, the pressure also plays a unique role since different structural MOFs were obtained under different reaction pressure [53]. Thus far, the most important issue is how to maintain the integrity of the building blocks [54]. Usually, this task is carried out by precipitation of the target product from the reacting solution.
Since then, the solvothermal method has become an effective approach to obtain new MOF structures, and many MOF families, such as MOF-74 [55], MOF-177 [56], MIL-101 [57], UiO-66 [58], and PCNs [59] have been prepared by this method.
Various new MOFs have been synthesized by this method. For example, Andreo and coworkers reported a novel thorium(IV)- MOF constructed from thorium nitrate and 2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (2,6-NDC) [60]. This highly stable MOF was synthesized via a solvothermal reaction and showed an
interesting topology caused by a new structural arrangement. This MOF shows excellent thermal stability and can maintain stable in air up to \(450\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Zaworotko's team demonstrated an exchange of the mode of entanglement in a square lattice (sql) by using different solvents during solvothermal synthesis [61]. The product \(\left[\mathrm{MIL}_{3}\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}\right]_{n}\) \(\left(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{C}_{0},\mathrm{Ni}\right)\) was constructed by an extended ligand, \(\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{2}\) -(1,4-phenylenebis(methanol-1-yl-1-ylidene))bis(4-(1H-imidazol-1-yl)aniline). The use of DMF/MeOH enables a \(2\mathrm{D}\rightarrow\) 3D parallel polycatenation whereas the use of nitrobenzene/MeOH causes a \(2\mathrm{D}\rightarrow2\mathrm{D}\) parallel interpenetration (Fig. 11).
Dinca et al. showed the synthesis of three La(III)-MOFs based on a tetrathiafulvalene linker [62]. By varying the synthetic conditions used in solvothermal synthesis, such as the solvent ratios and reaction temperature, these three MOFs showed distinct topologies.
Synthesizing MOFs from diverse building blocks, such as different heterometallic clusters or mixed linkers is still a great challenge. Muldoon et al. designed and synthesized a series of heterobimetallic MOFs by one-pot solvothermal reactions [63].
The eight obtained MOFs showed three different topologies, including a new trinodal pfm topology. Fischer and co-workers demonstrated the design and synthesis of the first anionic AlMOF, which was constructed by tetrahedral \(\mathrm{AlO_{4}}\) sites [64]. This crystalline Al-MOF was obtained by a two-step solvothermal route and formed a 3D network with 1D chains; notably, it presented the highest \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) loading and proved to be a potential single-ion \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) solid electrolyte.
Although numerous new MOFs have been obtained by solvothermal synthesis, limitations still exist, especially in large-scale production. For example, a longer reaction time (several hours to several days), a large amount of solvent, and prohibitive costs may be needed for up-scaling. It is difficult to guarantee the crystallinity, consistency, and yield of the products when the synthetic conditions are scaled up [65]. In the following sections, we will describe promising methods to successfully optimize reaction conditions that could be applicable to large-scale production.
## 3.2 Microwave-assisted synthesis
MW heating has been utilized in organic chemistry for a long time [66]. This method is based on the interaction between electromagnetic radiation and any material containing mobile electric charges, such as polar molecules in a solvent or conductive ions in a solid [65]. Therefore, water and ionic liquids can be promising solvents for microwave-assisted synthesis.
The common process of MW methods is described as follows: A mixture that contains reaction precursors and suitable solvents
is transferred to a sealed Teflon vessel or glass vessel, placed in an MW chamber and heated for the appropriate time.
The first work on use of the MW method to obtain MOFs was reported by Jhung et al. in 2005 [67]. Chromium trimesate (MIL100) was prepared by using MW-assisted hydrothermal synthesis. The reactants were added to a sealed Teflon autoclave, placed in a microwave oven and heated at \(220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The crystal yield of MIL100 by the MW method for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\) is \(44\%\) , which is comparable with the yield of \(45\%\) by conventional hydrothermal synthesis for 4 days. The extremely rapid synthesis of MIL-53(Al) only takes \(4.3\;\mathrm{s}\) [89]. In contrast to conventional heating, where thermal energy is transferred from the surroundings to reactants through thermal conduction and convection, MW heating has a faster reaction time because the radiation directly interacts with the reactants [65]. The significant reduction in reaction time makes MW synthesis a promising candidate for large-scale synthesis in some industrial applications because of its high efficiency and low energy consumption.
In addition, MW synthesis can control the size and morphology of MOF particles. A narrow particle size distribution is observed. The reduction in crystal size and narrow size distribution are attributed to the faster kinetics of nucleation and crystal growth caused by hot-spots [65]. For instance, the rapid synthesis of IRMOF-1, IRMOF-2, and IRMOF-3 via the MW method was reported by Masel et al., who discovered that the obtained MOFs exhibited a uniform crystal size and unique cubic morphology [90].
To control the particle size and morphology of MOF crystals,
researchers have also introduced modulators in the MW process. Liu et al. first synthesized micro- and nanometer-sized γ-cyclodextrin MOFs (γ-CD-MOFs) by a MW-assisted method [91]. Polyethylene glycol 20000 was selected as the modulator to adjust the size and morphology of γ-CD-MOFs (Fig. 12). Compared with micrometer-sized crystals, the nanometer-sized γ-CD-MOF presented faster and higher adsorption of Fenbufen.
Jiang et al., synthesized a series of porphyrin-based, 2D lanthanide-MOFs by using a household microwave oven rapidly and facilely [92]. The obtained Ln-MOF nanosheets showed different thickness- and thickness-dependent photocatalytic properties.
To understand the accelerated synthesis by MW, several studies compared the MW method with conventional heating and ultrasonic synthesis. MIL-53(Fe) was synthesized by Jhuang and co-workers by conventional electric (CE) heating, irradiation under MW, and ultrasound conditions [93]. By investigating the kinetics of nucleation and crystal growth processes, the authors
determined that the nucleation and growth rates decrease in the order: US > MW ≫CE, which demonstrated the significance of physical effects such as hot spots in the accelerated synthesis induced by MW and US.
precursors is fitted to a sonicator bar with adaptable ultrasonic power [96]. The MOF products will be obtained after several minutes or hours without using any cooling system. Different from MW-heating which depends on microwaves, sonochemical synthesis depends on acoustic cavitation instead of acoustic waves. With the sequential formation, growth, and collapse of bubbles (hot spots) in the solution, the reaction system obtained very high local temperatures \(\left(\sim5000\;\mathrm{K}\right)\), high pressure \(\left(\sim1000\;\mathrm{atm}\right)\), and unique heating/cooling rates \(\left(\sim10^{10}\,\mathrm{K}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\right)\) [97].
The pioneering work on the sonochemical method of MOFs by Qiu and co-workers reported the synthesis of a fluorescence microporous MOF, \(\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) in 2008 [98]. The solution of zinc acetate and linker \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) was introduced to a reactor under ultrasonic irradiation at ambient temperature and pressure.
High-yield products were obtained after only \(5\;\mathrm{min}\), and the yield increased over the reaction time.
Compared with conventional synthesis methods, the sonochemical method proves to be superior in terms of reduced reaction time, simplicity, and energy efficiency [99]. Several works have reported the design and synthesis of ultrathin MOF nanosheets via sonochemical methods. The first method was reported by Tang's team in 2016 [100]. A sealed polytetrafluoroethylene (PE) tube with a mixture of metal salts, linkers, and solvents was continuously ultrasonicated for \(8\,\mathrm{~h~}\) \((40\ \mathrm{kHz})\) to obtain NiCo bimetal organic framework nanosheets (NiCo-UMOFNs). The as-synthesized NiCo-UMOFNs loaded on copper foam presented superior electrochemical activity toward the oxygen evolution reaction. In 2017, Li et al. also fabricated ultrathin Mn- and Ni-based MOF nanosheets via a rapid and versatile ultrasonic method [101]. Mn-UMOFNs possessed a thinner thickness, smaller metal-ion radius, and higher specific surface area than Ni-UMOFNs and therefore presented a high reversible capacity and fast Li-ions diffusion coefficient as anode materials for Li-ion batteries. Another example reported by the same group demonstrated the facile synthesis of ultrathin Co-based MOF nanosheets through the one-pot sonochemical method [102]. Compared with its bulk products, Co-UMOFNs exhibited a high-rate capacity and excellent reversible capacity in Li-ion batteries. In 2019, ultrathin MOF nanosheets based on Co, Ni, and Mn were synthesized and employed as cathode electrocatalysts in \(\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) batteries by Yuan et al. [103].
Different ultrasound powers can gain MOFs with various structures and/or morphologies. For instance, the CuTATB- \(n\), \((\mathrm{TATB}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}\)-s-triazine-2,4,6-triyltribenzoate; \(n=\) power level) with either a catenated (CuTATB-60) structure or non-catenated (CuTATB-30) structure was synthesized by a simple and rapid sonochemical route. As shown in Fig. 13, the mixture of metal salts and organic linkers was introduced to a horn-type Pyrex
reactor fitted to a sonicator bar under a helium gas environment for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The catenated (CuTATB-60) structure was obtained by \(60\%\) ultrasonic power (300 W), while the non-catenated (CuTATB-30) structure was synthesized by \(30\%\) ultrasonic power (150 W). Such fine-tuning of catenation was further verified by the synthesis of IRMOF-9 (catenated) and IRMOF-10 (non-catenated) through the same ultrasonic strategy [104].
A novel zinc-based coordination polymer with a flower-like morphology was synthesized by the sonochemical method. The flower-like structure of \(\{[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{I})_{0.5}(1,4\mathrm{-bib})]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}=1,2,4,5\cdot\) - cyclohexanetetracarboxylic acid, \(1,4\mathrm{-bib}\,=\,1,4\mathrm{-bis}(1\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-imidazol-1-}\) yl)benzene) was prepared by placing the mixture of precursors under ultrasonic irradiation for \(80\,\mathrm{\min}\), while the single crystal was obtained by the hydrothermal reaction at \(140\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days [105].
## 3.4 Electrochemical synthesis
Electrochemical synthesis utilizes electric energy to initiate the redox reaction during MOF synthesis. There are two types of electrochemical synthesis of MOFs: (1) the anodic dissolution and (2) the cathodic deposition [65]. In the first type, metal ions are continuously generated from the dissolution of the anode under an applied electric potential and then reacted with the organic linkers dissolved in the solution. The obtained MOF products could be powders or thin films. For instance, the first patent by BASF demonstrated the rapid synthesis of HKUST-1 [106]. In this patent, the copper plate, which acted as the electrode, was immersed in the solution containing the linker \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\). The powder of HKUST-1 was synthesized after applying a current of \(1.3\mathrm{~A~}\) and a voltage of \(12{-}19\ \mathrm{V}\). In the cathodic deposition, the reactant solution contains metal ions, organic linkers, and probase. The reaction is generated due to the increasing \(\mathrm{pH}\) near the cathode, which causes the deposition of MOF thin films. The first example of cathodic deposition of MOFs was reported by Dinca's team in 2011, who synthesized MOF-5 within only \(15\,\mathrm{\min}\) at room temperature (RT) [70].
The electrochemical synthesis of MOFs has attracted great attention owing to many advantages. First, the synthesis usually requires milder synthetic conditions (such as room temperature and ambient pressure) and shorter reaction times than conventional synthesis. As reported by Wei and co-workers, rapid and large-scale synthesis of IRMOF-3 was carried out through the electrochemical method at room temperature [107]. Further study of a Zr-based MOF, \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), was delivered by the same team [108]. The team selected Zr metal as the metal source, which dissolved under an applied voltage. A mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\)-MOF, MFM-100, was demonstrated by Kang et al. via ionic liquid-templated electrosynthesis in only \(100\,\mathrm{~s~}\) at room temperature [109]. The products were obtained by using copper foil as both the anode and cathode. In addition, the morphology, structure, porosity, and crystal defects of MFM-100 can be tuned by using ionic liquids as templates.
Second, since metal ions can be obtained by anodic dissolution
of metals, metal salts containing toxic and hard-to-separate anions (such as \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) or \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)) can be avoided. Therefore, electrochemical synthesis could be a promising candidate for green and large-scale MOF manufacturing.
Last but not least, MOF thin films can be easily fabricated by electrosynthesis, especially the cathodic deposition. The classic two-electrode system is suitable for synthesis that directly uses the potential difference between the working electrode and the counter electrode to initiate the reaction (Figs. 14(a) and 14(b)). For the three-electrode system (Fig. 14(c)), due to the reference electrode, the potential difference between the working electrode and the counter electrode can be precisely measured and controlled [110].
Li et al. reported a facile electrochemical synthesis of fluorescent MOF films \((\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2})\) [111]. Voltage and reaction time are determined to be the main effective factors for the formation and morphology of the prepared MOF films. Dinca's group presented a biphasic MOF thin film synthesized by potential-controlled cathodic deposition [112]. They selected \(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{NH}^{+}/\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) as the \(\mathrm{pH}\) buffer pair and proved that the biphasic MOF thin film can be obtained by controlling the \(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{NH}^{+}\)concentration.
Liu and co-workers presented the preparation of a 2D \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{HHTP}\,=\,2,3,6,7,10,11\)-hexahydroxytriphenylene) MOF film that grew on \(\mathrm{Cu}(100)\) foil by a controllable electrochemical method (Fig. 15). The obtained MOF film exhibited high crystalline quality and high electrical conductivity up to \(0.087\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), which was 1000 times larger than those of MOF films prepared by liquid interface methods [113].
Zhou et al. reported a directed-assembly strategy using an electrochemical method to fabricate polycrystalline MOF membranes. A series of defect-free MOF membranes with different metal clusters and/or ligands were obtained. Among them, Zr-fum-fcu-MOF and Y-fum-fcu-MOF (fum = fumarate) membranes exhibited great separation of propane/propylene and butane/isobutane, respectively [114].
## 3.5 Mechanochemical synthesis
Mechanochemical synthesis depends on the mechanical force, which is attributed to the grinding or milling of precursor solids. In the general synthetic route of the ball-milling process, reaction precursors are introduced into a ball-mill grinder and continuously milled at an applied frequency. Through mechanosynthesis, reactions with minimal amounts of solvents or without any solvents could be achieved [65]. Therefore, mechanosynthesis has been recognized as a "green" and energy-efficient synthetic approach. In addition, mechanosynthesis introduces the possibility of using metal sources with low solubility, such as metals, metal oxides, hydroxides, carbonates, and acetates, and when metal oxides and hydroxides are selected, the reaction only produces water as the byproduct, which can be removed by simple heating [65].
According to the previous classification, there are three main approaches [65]: the solvent-free grinding (SFG) method, which
operates without solvent and is the simplest method; liquid-assisted grinding (LAG), which uses a small amount of liquid to enhance the mobility of the reactants and is quicker and more pervasive; finally, ion-and-liquid assisted grinding (ILAG), which uses catalytic liquids containing ions to promote the formation of MOFs. The mechanochemical method can also be categorized as grinding, ball-milling, and extruding methods. In this section, we mainly focus on the ball-milling method and extrusion due to their wider application and easier shaping and large-scale production, especially for extrusion.
## 3.5.1 Ball-milling method
The first example of mechanochemical synthesis of MOFs was reported by James' team in 2006 [115]. This pioneering work demonstrated the synthesis of a microporous MOF, \(\mathrm{[Cu(INA)_{2}]}\) (INA = isonicotinic acid) under solvent-free mechanochemical grinding. A ball mill was used to simultaneously grind the copper acetate and isonicotinic acid without any solvent and the MOF products were obtained within \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\). The byproducts (water and acetic acid) can be removed by direct heating, and a quantitative yield was obtained.
Following this strategy, our group designed and synthesized
ZIF-8 with trapped polyoxometallates in the cages (denoted POM@MOFs) [116]. The three obtained compounds, \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) \(\subset\) ZIF-8 (BIT-1), \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SiW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\subset\) ZIF-8 (BIT-2), and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) \(\subset\) ZIF-8 (BIT-3), were prepared by solid-state ball-milling of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) and 2-methylimidazole in \(30\,\mathrm{min}\).
Metal clusters can also be employed in mechanochemical synthesis. Fidelli et al. synthesized two \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs from dodecanuclear zirconium acetate clusters [117]. This is the first time an NU-type MOF has been reported via a solvent-free method. Karadeniz and co-workers used the same dodecanuclear zirconium acetate clusters as the precursor to investigate how mechanosynthesis controlled the structural transformation in porphyrinic \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs [118]. The reaction was governed by milling additives and the zirconium precursor, and the pure phase of hexagonal PCN-223 and cubic MOF-525 phases were obtained.
Ayoub et al. presented a rapid and efficient mechanochemistry method to synthesize 12 microporous MM' -MOF-74 \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\), Mg, Ni, Cu, and Co; \(\mathbf{M}^{\prime}\,=\,\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\), Co, Ca, Mg, and Cu) with a predetermined 1:1 stoichiometric ratio [81]. As shown in Fig. 16, only mechanosynthesis can control the stoichiometry of MM' - MOF-74 compared with the conventional method. Very recently, Nawrocki and co-workers reported HKUST-1 with encapsulation
of ibuprofen (IBU) [75]. The one-pot reaction started from a \(\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(I B U)_{4}]{\cdot}2D M F}\) cluster that was utilized not only as the precursor but also for in-situ integration of ibuprofen within the HKUST-1 structure.
Lee et al. demonstrated a mechanochemical method to construct four well-known MOFs (MOF-5, MOF-177, UiO-67, and ZIF-65) by ball milling. All four MOFs could be easily reconstituted through the mechanochemical process which confirms the possibility of recycling MOFs [119].
## 3.5.2 Extrusion method
Grinding and milling methods under solvent-free conditions are widely employed as rapid and simple methods for MOF synthesis. However, the production rates of these batch processing techniques still need to be improved. Extrusion is a continuous process in which the reaction precursors are forced through confined spaces. A typical extrusion line is composed of a feeder part and the heating barrel which contains either single screw extrusion (SSE) or twin screw extrusion (TSE) to convey the products. A mold can be installed at the exit to shape the products [120]. The extrusion method can be recognized as a continuous manufacturing approach for industrial production.
In 2015, James's group employed twin screw extruders to prepare three typical MOFs, HKUST-1, ZIF-8, and Al(fumarate)(OH) for the first time (Fig. 17) [76]. This process not only showed a quantitative yield at a \(\mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) rate but also produced MOFs with both pore volumes and surface areas equivalent to those of MOFs obtained by conventional solvothermal methods. Notably, the maximum STYs of the extrusion method are \(144\times\) \(10^{3}\ \mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-3}\cdot\mathrm{d}^{-1}\), which demonstrates that this method is a viable approach to scale-up MOF production.
Karadeniz et al. presented a green and scalable synthesis of a series of Zr-MOFs (UiO-66, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), MOF-801, and MOF-804) by a water-assisted extrusion method [83]. The use of the non-conventional dodecanuclear zirconium cluster as the precursor affords high-performance Zr-MOFs within \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The synthesis by TSE yielded more than \(100\ \mathrm{g}\) of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) in a continuous route with a rate of \(1.4\,\mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\).
Darwish et al. compared the mechanochemistry with the solution method and slurry for the synthesis of five coordination polymers formed by \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\)-bipyridine and metal ions [121]. Both the ball-milling and TSE method rapidly realized high-yield and low
waste products, whereas the water slurry process could not achieve these products.
Abramova and co-workers reported an extrusion-spheronization process to prepare highly porous and robust granules by using UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH\(_2\) as MOF powders and hydroxyethyl cellulose as binder. The shaped MOFs retained their physical/chemical properties and showed improved mechanical strength [122].
Quan et al. demonstrated a sustainable and solvent-free approach to directly obtain nanocomposites from MOF and polymer matrixes via the extrusion method for the first time (Fig. 18). ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 were chosen as MOFs. Polypropylene and polystyrene were chosen as polymer matrixes to investigate the feasibility and universality of the extrusion method. STYs reached \(1.2\,\times\,10^{5}\,\mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-3}\cdot\mathrm{d}^{-1}\), which provides a sustainable and efficient approach to scale up MOF composites [123].
## 3.6 Hot-pressing synthesis
The HoP method, in which pressure and temperature are simultaneously onto desired substrates, can be applied to fabricate MOF nanocrystals in solvent-free conditions or with a minimal amount of solvents [71]. Figure 19 shows the two steps of the HoP method. First, metal ions or organic linkers were bonded to the surface of substrates with the functional groups or metal sites on it under the given temperature and pressure; then, nucleation and consequent crystal growth were initiated [124].
In 2016, our group first demonstrated the HoP method for obtaining MOF coatings [71]. This is a solvent- and binder-free approach that can produce various MOF coatings within \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) under applied temperature and pressure. This simple and rapid synthetic strategy proved to be applicable to either carboxylate and imidazolate linkers or mixed metal ions to produce versatile MOF coatings, including MOF-5, Co-MOF, Cd-MOF, ZIF-8, ZIF-9, ZIF-67, and Ni-ZIF-8 (Fig. 20). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, elemental mapping images, and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns confirmed the successful fabrication of MOF coatings. Notably, MOF can be coated on six flexible substrates via the same process, including carbon cloth (CC), nickel foam, anodic aluminum oxide film, copper foil, glass fiber, and glass cloth. The layer-by-layer method and the obtained "Janus" films further broaden the application of HoP. In addition, the advanced roll-to-roll HoP method demonstrated the feasibility
for scale-up mass production in industrial manufacturing.
Later, we utilized the roll-to-roll HoP method to mass produce MOFilters based on five flexible commercial substrates (glass cloth, plastic mesh, nonwoven fabric, metal mesh, and melamine foam) [125]. The contained MOFs, including ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and Ni-ZIF-8, were confirmed by PXRD, SEM, and elemental mapping characterization. The MOF loadings and MOF particle sizes could be tuned by changing the HoP cycles. The obtained MOFilters exhibited excellent performance in particulate matter (PM) removal with long-term (at least 30 days) duration and recycling. Therefore, the roll-to-roll HoP method can be applied to residential pollution control.
Tang et al. reported a solvent-free HoP method for synthesizing polyoxometalate-based MOFs (POMOFs) on CC [126]. The obtained POMOFs/CC can be transformed into bimetallic phosphides (Cu-M-P/CC, \(\mathrm{~\textit~{~M~}~}=\mathrm{~\textit~{~M~}~}\) or W) on CC via a phosphorization process. Li and co-workers demonstrated a novel coating strategy through the solid-phase HoP method to coat InMOF (InOF-1) in situ on different fabrics, such as cotton, polyester, and aramid. The obtained coatings showed excellent performance in ultraviolet (UV)-blocking with slight robustness
loss (Fig. 21) [127].
Recently, our group investigated the photocatalytic bactericidal properties of several MOFs and then applied the solvent-free HoP method to fabricate the corresponding MOFilters [128]. The method based on ZIF-8 showed high efficiency for both photocatalytic killing against airborne bacteria \((>99.99\%)\) and PM removal \((>97\%)\), which could be applied to integrated personal and environmental protection (Fig. 22).
In addition to MOF thin films, the HoP method can also be utilized in the fabrication of mixed matrix membranes (MMMs). Our group presented a thermally induced phase separation-hot pressing (TIPS-HoP) approach for the scalable production of various MOF membranes [129]. The TIPS-HoP method combined with the roll-to-roll process successfully prepared 10 different MOF membranes with high MOF loadings (up to \(86\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\)). These membranes exhibited robust mechanical strength and high flux separation performance on account of ultrahigh-molecular-weight polyethylene and their hierarchical porous structure, respectively. Under the cross-flow filtration mode, an ultrahigh flux and rejection rate as well as capture of small dye molecules for water treatment were achieved. Moreover, by using
different MOFs, enantiomers and proteins can be separated with high solvent permeance and selectivity in a dead-end filtration mode. Hence, the TIPS-HoP method is very promising for large-scale, roll-to-roll production of MOF membranes, which could be applied in existing industrial facilities.
## 3.7 Spray-drying synthesis
The SD method has been proven to be a low-cost and continuous scalable approach used in the production of medicine, food products, pigments, and organic and inorganic compounds for decades. This method is based on the rapid evaporation of precursors with a hot gas [130].
Using the SD method to produce MOFs was developed by Maspoch's team for the first time [77]. The solution of MOF precursors containing both metal ions and linkers was atomized into microdroplets through a two-fluid nozzle spraying dryer (Fig. 23). The solution was then injected at a certain rate and the compressed air or nitrogen gas was simultaneously injected at another rate. Each microdroplet was suspended by a heated gas stream. This process led to the evaporation of solvents and diffusion of precursor ions or molecules to the droplet surface. The supersaturation of reactant solutions occurred due to the shrinking of droplets and then nano-MOF crystals began crystallizing and packing at the droplet surface. After this series of reactions, spherical hollow superstructures were obtained. Notably, 14 different nano-MOFs were synthesized by this strategy.
Subsequently, SD synthesis has been extended to various MOF families such as MILs, UiOs, and ZIFs. However, the obtained MOFs with high-nuclearity secondary building units (SBUs) showed low yield and/or poor sorption properties. To solve these problems, an advanced technique combining the SD method with the continuous-flow method was reported by the same group in 2016 [131]. Several new MOFs, including the UiO series, FeBTC/MIL-100, and \(\mathrm{[Ni_{8}(OH)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{6}]_{n}}\) \(\mathrm{~\textit~{~L~}~}=~1H\) -pyrazole-4- carboxylic acid) were synthesized and shaped in microspherical superstructures (beads).
showed low yield and/or poor sorption properties. To solve these problems, an advanced technique combining the SD method with the continuous-flow method was reported by the same group in 2016 [131]. Several new MOFs, including the UiO series, FeBTC/MIL-100, and \(\mathrm{[Ni_{8}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{6}]_{n}}\) \(\mathrm{~\textit~{~L~}~}=~1H\) -pyrazole-4- carboxylic acid) were synthesized and shaped in microspherical superstructures (beads).
The synthesis of high-quality ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and \(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co-ZIF}\) through the SD strategy was reported by Chaemchuen and coworkers [87], who discussed the mechanism of MOF crystal growth during the SD process. The authors noted the following findings: The PXRD pattern of the precipitate of the feed solution matched that of layered basic zinc acetate, an intermediate amorphous phase was formed after spray drying the feed solution, and crystalline ZIF structures were obtained after immersion in a solvent. However, no crystalline product was obtained from those two processes, and the crystalline ZIF-8 particles can only be taken by solvent immersion of spherical products.
Avci-Camur et al. demonstrated the green and rapid synthesis of two Zr-MOFs \(\mathrm{(UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) and MOF-801) by a continuous-flow SD method under aqueous conditions [84]. High-quality MOF beads with high surface areas and water sorption capacity were obtained by controlling the concentration of acetic acid in water.
Avci and co-workers reported a green, low-cost, and high throughput synthesis of micrometric-sized photonic balls via the SD method. The authors mainly focused on ZIF-8 colloids due to their stability and sorption capability. ZIF-67 and ZIF-8/Au nanoparticles were also fabricated by the same process to verify the generalizability of this SD method [132].
Compared with solvothermal methods, the spray-drying
method can reduce reaction time and achieve a continuous process for scale-up production. However, this strategy often results in poorly crystalline MOFs due to the extremely short nucleation and crystallization time. Sun et al. promoted the SD method to synthesize high-quality HKUST-1 by using dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) as a co-solvent since they discovered that using solvents with high donor numbers and low vapor pressures could increase crystallization and solvent evaporation times [133]. As shown in Fig. 24, the precursor solutions can be stabilized by the premixing process, and the mixture of DMSO and DMF can reduce the evaporation rate.
Additionally, chemical vapor deposition (CVD) which is based on the reaction between vaporized precursors and substrate can also form uniform MOF thin films or MOF coatings [134]. Both SD and CVD methods are simple and are promising candidate technologies for industrial application that benefits from the high space time yield. However, the reaction of CVD usually occurs on the gas–solid interfaces, which differs from SD occurring on the gas–gas interfaces. CVD is more suitable for preparing MOF thin film, high-loading MOF coatings, and metal-decorated MOF composite (M@MOF) films.
## 3.8 Continuous flow synthesis
Continuous flow synthesis, in which reactant solutions are pumped by syringe, high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), or peristaltic pumps through microfluidic chips or reactor coils in a continuous process [135], can be divided into three categories: microfluidic reactors (MRs), in which reactant solutions are operated in channels that are constrained at the microscale; plug flow reactors (PFRs), in which reagents are pumped through pipes or tubes; stirred tank reactors (CSTRs), in which the reagents are introduced to the tanks [65].
Compared with conventional batch synthetic methods, continuous flow synthesis takes advantage of a much more rapid reaction, more precise control of reaction parameters especially the reaction time, the use of less solvent, lower energy consumption, and greater suitability for scale-up. The first example of continuous flow synthesis was reported by Ameloot and co-workers where HKUST-1 was synthesized by microfluidic method. MOF crystals were synthesized in a microscale reactor in which the reactant solution was injected into an immiscibility fluid to obtain HKUST-1 microcapsules [78].
Polyzoidis et al. reported a continuous synthesis method of ZIF8 using a T-type micromixer (Fig. 25). The ZIF-8 product was obtained with a theoretical STY that reached \(210,000\;\mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-3}\cdot\mathrm{d}^{-1}\). It is possible to control both the morphology and crystal size of ZIF8 by varying the reaction solvents and aging time [88].
Nevertheless, to scale up the synthesis of MOFs, the use of larger reactors, such as stirred tank reactors, is optimal. Mckinstry and co-workers presented the synthesis of MOF-5 by a continuous flow method in an atmosphere with a STY of \(1000\;\mathrm{kg}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-3}\cdot\mathrm{d}^{-1}\) [72].
Several studies have reported cases that combine continuous flow techniques and other methods, for example, MW-assisted methods. Albuquerque et al. demonstrated a rapid and continuous flow microwave-assisted synthesis of MOF-74(Ni) with a high STY and high conversion of reagents. In this study, researchers used gas–liquid segmented flow to induce fast nucleation, which accelerated the MOF synthetic rates [136].
Using the same continuous flow process, Taddei and coworkers presented a versatile and highly efficient strategy to obtain MOFs such as HKUST-1, MIL-53(AI), and UiO-66 [85]. As shown in Fig. 26, benchmark materials were synthesized with high space-time yield and remarkable mass. The synthesis was extended to multigram production within several minutes.
Different application scenarios, especially the industrial
applications put forward various requirements for the morphology, particle size, hardness, shape, mechanical strength, etc. of MOFs. The obtained MOF are usually crystalline fine powders or millimeter-sized crystals. However, crystalline powders or small crystals are not industrially favorable due to their limitations in processing, handling, and storing, and they are also difficult to be regenerated or reconstructed for recycling [137]. Therefore, one of the available methods is shaping MOFs into different forms while preserving or improving their properties. Synthetic methods including MC method (especially extrusion) and SD method can provide shaped MOF products with extremely high STY. Alternatively, using EC method and HoP method can obtain homogenous MOF coatings, thin films, or membranes. Additionally, in catalysis, the morphology and particle size of the MOFs can also affect the catalytic performance, and the microwave method and ultrasonic method can control the particle size and morphology effectively.
Considering that the industrialization of MOFs is accelerating, the cost of each method must be considered. There are a number of factors that need to be taken into account: 1) Compared with batch processing, the continuous one including MW, HoP, SD, and CF methods can deliver higher products per unit time and an incessant production; 2) the price of reaction precursors, such as metal ions, organic ligands, and solvent, needs to be measured, and therefore, some solvent-free or less solvent used methods, including MC, HoP, and CF will be more economical; 3) a mild reaction condition is possible to reduce operating costs, by which MC and CF methods can be achieved; 4) a high STY is necessary, such as MC, CF, and SD methods.
## 4 Conclusion and perspective
The progress and advances of several synthetic strategies of MOF products, including conventional solvothermal, microwave-assisted, sonochemical, electrochemical, mechanochemical, hot-pressing, spray-drying, and continuous flow methods, have been discussed. Compared with conventional synthesis, the other
synthetic methods allow an unprecedented level both in the precise control of reaction conditions and for the fine-turned sizes and morphologies of MOF products. Notably, hot-pressing methods, spray-drying synthesis, and continuous flow synthesis which are rapid and continuous methods in mass production, are the promising candidates for industrial application.
Although excellent progress has been achieved, MOF fabrication, which means the design and synthesis of MOF products from preconceived metal ions/clusters and organic linkers, still meets great challenges. Future studies will focus on those four aspects: 1) investigating the coordination mode, topology design, and the relationship between reaction parameters and MOF products structure; 2) proving the generality of these methods, since they are only suitable for certain MOFs with mild reaction conditions; 3) expanding the applicability of those synthetic methods which are suitable for the industry application so that they can be applied simultaneously to the development of new structures; 4) exploring post-treatment and activation methods with simpler routes and lower energy consumption of MOF products.
